window.LEARN_CONTENT=window.LEARN_CONTENT||{};window.LEARN_CONTENT["a-d955137d94c4d306"]={"zh":"<p><a id=\"worktrees\"></a></p> <h1 id=\"worktree\">工作树（Worktree）</h1> <blockquote> <p>完整文档目录见 <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt（需联网）</a>。在文档页面的 URL 末尾添加 <code>.md</code>，即可获取 Markdown 版本。</p> </blockquote> <p>工作树（Worktree）允许 Codex 在同一个项目中运行多个彼此独立的对话，互不干扰。代码仓库、工作树和命令都保留在项目所在的电脑或远程开发环境中。你可以直接在 ChatGPT 桌面应用中操作，也可以在 ChatGPT 手机应用中使用 <a href=\"#/article/a-e49d01f23299a44c\">Codex</a>，在已连接的电脑上启动、指导、批准并检查工作树对话。</p> <p>对于 Git 仓库，<a href=\"#/article/a-b5406e60cbfa0b68\">定时任务</a>可以在专用的后台工作树中运行，避免与你正在进行的工作冲突。对于未使用版本控制的项目，定时任务会直接在项目目录中运行。你也可以手动在工作树中启动对话，并使用“移交”（Handoff）在“本地”（Local）与“工作树”（Worktree）之间移动对话。</p> <p>工作树不会在手机本机运行。ChatGPT 手机应用控制的是 已连接电脑上的 Codex；代码仓库和工作树仍保留在那里，或保留在该电脑使用的远程开发环境中。下面针对桌面应用的说明适用于这台已连接的电脑。</p> <p><a id=\"whats-a-worktree\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_1\">什么是工作树</h2> <p>工作树只能用于 Git 仓库中的项目，因为其底层使用了 <a href=\"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-worktree\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Git worktree（需联网）</a>。工作树允许你为仓库创建第二份工作副本（一次“检出”）。每个工作树都有一份仓库文件副本，但它们共享提交、分支等元数据（<code>.git</code> 文件夹）。因此，你可以同时检出多个分支并并行开展工作。</p> <p><a id=\"terminology\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_2\">术语说明</h2> <ul> <li><strong>本地检出（Local checkout）</strong>：你创建的代码仓库。在 ChatGPT 桌面应用中，有时简称为 <strong>Local（本地）</strong>。</li> <li><strong>工作树（Worktree）</strong>：在 ChatGPT 桌面应用中基于本地检出创建的 <a href=\"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-worktree\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Git 工作树（需联网）</a>。</li> <li><strong>移交（Handoff）</strong>：在 Local（本地）与 Worktree（工作树）之间移动对话的流程。Codex 会处理必要的 Git 操作，安全地在两者之间移动你的工作。</li> </ul> <p><a id=\"why-use-a-worktree\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_3\">为什么要使用工作树</h2> <ol> <li>与 Codex 并行工作，同时不影响当前的 Local（本地）环境。</li> <li>将任务排入后台处理，同时继续专注于前台工作。</li> <li>准备好检查、测试或更直接地协作时，再将对话移到 Local（本地）。</li> </ol> <p><a id=\"getting-started\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_4\">开始使用</h2> <p>工作树要求项目位于 Git 仓库中。请先确认你选择的项目属于某个 Git 仓库。</p> <ol> <li>选择“Worktree（工作树）”</li> </ol> <p>在新对话页面中，在输入框下方选择 <strong>Worktree（工作树）</strong>。 你也可以选择一个<a href=\"#/article/a-86cde8be2e24d05a\">本地环境</a>，以便为工作树运行设置脚本。</p> <ol> <li>选择起始分支</li> </ol> <p>在输入框下方选择用于创建工作树的 Git 分支。它可以是 <code>main</code> / <code>master</code> 分支、功能分支，也可以是包含尚未暂存的本地更改的当前分支。</p> <ol> <li>提交提示</li> </ol> <p>提交提示后，Codex 会基于你选择的分支创建 Git 工作树。默认情况下，Codex 会在<a href=\"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-checkout#_detached_head\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">“分离 HEAD”（需联网）</a>状态下工作。</p> <ol> <li>选择后续工作位置</li> </ol> <p>准备好后，你可以继续直接在工作树中工作，也可以把对话移交到本地检出。从 Local（本地）移入或移出时，对话_和_代码都会一起移动，这样你就能在另一个检出环境中继续工作。</p> <p><a id=\"working-between-local-and-worktree\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"local-worktree\">在 Local（本地）与 Worktree（工作树）之间切换</h2> <p>工作树的外观和使用方式与本地检出很相似，区别在于它们在工作流程中的位置。你可以把 Local（本地）看作前台，把 Worktree（工作树）看作后台。“移交”（Handoff）功能可以在两者之间移动对话。</p> <p>在底层，“移交”（Handoff）会执行必要的 Git 操作，确保工作能在两个检出环境之间安全移动。这一点很重要，因为 <strong>Git 只允许一个分支同时在一个位置被检出</strong>。如果你已在某个工作树中检出一个分支，就<strong>不能</strong>同时在本地检出同一个分支，反过来也一样。</p> <p>实际使用中，常见的方式有两种：</p> <ol> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-d955137d94c4d306?anchor=option-1-working-on-the-worktree\">只在工作树中工作</a>。如果你可以直接在工作树中验证更改，这种方式最合适；例如，你已通过<a href=\"#/article/a-86cde8be2e24d05a\">本地环境设置脚本</a>安装好所需依赖和工具。</li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-d955137d94c4d306?anchor=option-2-handing-a-chat-off-to-local\">将对话移交到 Local（本地）</a>。如果你想把对话带到前台，可以使用这种方式；例如，你想在常用 IDE 中检查更改，或者你的应用只能运行一个实例。</li> </ol> <p><a id=\"option-1-working-on-the-worktree\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"1\">方式 1：在工作树中工作</h3> <p>如果你想只在工作树中继续处理更改，请使用对话标题栏中的 <strong>Create branch here（在此处创建分支）</strong> 按钮，将工作树转换为一个分支。</p> <p>接下来，你可以提交更改、将分支推送到远程仓库，并在 GitHub 上创建拉取请求。</p> <p>你可以使用标题栏中的“Open（打开）”按钮，让 IDE 打开这个工作树；也可以使用集成终端，或在工作树目录中执行其他所需操作。</p> <blockquote> <p>插图：包含分支控件和工作树详情的工作树对话视图。</p> </blockquote> <p>请记住，如果你在某个工作树中创建了分支，就不能在任何其他工作树（包括本地检出）中检出该分支。</p> <p><a id=\"option-2-handing-a-thread-off-to-local\"></a> <a id=\"option-2-handing-a-chat-off-to-local\"></a> <a id=\"option-2-handing-a-task-off-to-local\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"2-local\">方式 2：将对话移交到 Local（本地）</h3> <p>如果你想把对话移到前台，请在对话标题栏中选择 <strong>Hand off（移交）</strong>，并将其移到 <strong>Local（本地）</strong>。</p> <p>如果你想在常用的 IDE 窗口中查看更改、运行现有开发服务器，或在日常使用的环境中验证工作，这种方式就很适合。</p> <p>Codex 会处理必要的 Git 步骤，安全地在工作树与本地检出之间移动对话。</p> <p>每个对话都会一直关联同一个工作树。如果之后你把对话移回工作树，Codex 会将其返回到同一个后台环境，让你从上次停下的地方继续。</p> <blockquote> <p>插图：将对话从工作树移交到 Local（本地）的对话框。</p> </blockquote> <p>你也可以反向操作。如果你当前在 Local（本地）工作，并想腾出前台，可以使用 <strong>Hand off（移交）</strong> 将对话移到工作树。当你希望 Codex 在后台继续工作，而自己转去处理本地的其他事情时，这种方式很有用。</p> <p>由于“移交”（Handoff）依赖 Git 操作，<code>.gitignore</code> 中列出的文件不会随对话一起移动；除非 Codex 根据 <code>.worktreeinclude</code> 将这些文件复制到本地托管的工作树中。</p> <p><a id=\"advanced-details\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_5\">高级说明</h2> <p><a id=\"codex-managed-and-permanent-worktrees\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"codex\">Codex 托管的工作树与永久工作树</h3> <p>默认情况下，对话使用由 Codex 托管的工作树。这类工作树设计得轻量且可临时使用，通常专用于一个对话；之后如果你把该对话移回工作树，Codex 会将其返回到同一个工作树。</p> <p>如果你需要长期保留某个环境，可以在侧边栏项目的三点菜单中创建永久工作树。这会创建一个独立项目形式的新永久工作树。永久工作树不会被自动删除，并且你可以从同一个工作树启动多个对话。</p> <p><a id=\"how-codex-manages-worktrees-for-you\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"codex_1\">Codex 如何管理工作树</h3> <p>Codex 会在 <code>$CODEX_HOME/worktrees</code> 中创建工作树。起始提交是你启动对话时所选分支的 <code>HEAD</code> 提交。如果你选择的分支包含本地更改，Codex 也会将未提交的更改应用到工作树中。工作树不会作为某个分支检出，而是处于<a href=\"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-checkout#_detached_head\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">分离 HEAD（需联网）</a>状态。这样，Codex 就能创建多个工作树，而不会让你的分支变得杂乱。</p> <p><a id=\"copy-ignored-local-files-into-managed-worktrees\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_6\">将被忽略的本地文件复制到托管工作树</h3> <p>本地 Codex 托管工作树从 Git 检出创建，因此已包含受 Git 跟踪的文件。如果仓库忽略了新工作树所需的本地设置文件，请在仓库根目录添加 <code>.worktreeinclude</code> 文件，并列出 Codex 创建托管工作树时应复制的被忽略路径或 <code>.gitignore</code> 风格的匹配模式。</p> <p>此文件适用于 Git 有意忽略的文件，例如 <code>.env</code>、<code>.env.local</code> 或 <code>config/secrets.json</code>。Codex 只会复制符合 <code>.worktreeinclude</code> 规则的被忽略文件；不会复制其他未受 Git 跟踪的本地文件。不要在其中列出受 Git 跟踪的文件。</p> <p>Codex 会自动将被忽略的 <code>AGENTS.override.md</code> 复制到本地托管工作树中，因此你无需在 <code>.worktreeinclude</code> 中列出它。</p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\"># .worktreeinclude\n.env\n.env.local\nconfig/secrets.json\n</code></pre> <p>Codex 会跳过源符号链接，也不会覆盖新检出环境中已存在的文件。此行为适用于 ChatGPT 桌面应用在本地托管的工作树；不适用于远程工作树，也不适用于你从命令行自行创建的 Git 工作树。</p> <p><a id=\"branch-limitations\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_7\">分支限制</h3> <p>假设 Codex 在某个工作树中完成了一些工作，而你通过 <strong>Create branch here（在此处创建分支）</strong> 在该工作树中创建了 <code>feature/a</code> 分支。现在你想在本地检出中试用它。如果你尝试检出该分支，就会看到以下错误：</p> <pre><code>fatal: 'feature/a' is already used by worktree at '&lt;WORKTREE_PATH&gt;'\n</code></pre> <p>要解决此问题，你需要在该工作树中检出其他分支，而不是 <code>feature/a</code>。</p> <p>如果你打算在本地检出该分支，请使用“移交”（Handoff）将对话移到 Local（本地），不要尝试让同一个分支同时在两个位置处于检出状态。</p> <p><strong>为什么会有此限制</strong></p> <p>Git 不允许同一个分支同时在多个工作树中检出，因为一个分支对应一个可变引用（<code>refs/heads/&lt;name&gt;</code>），其含义是某个工作树“当前检出的状态”。</p> <p>检出分支后，Git 会将该分支的 HEAD 视为由这个工作树持有，并要求提交、重置、变基和合并等操作按明确、有序的方式推进此引用。如果允许多个工作树同时检出同一个分支，就会不清楚究竟由哪个工作树更新分支引用，还会产生竞态条件，可能导致提交丢失、索引不一致或冲突处理不明确。</p> <p>通过实施“每个分支只能由一个工作树检出”的规则，Git 确保每个分支只有一份权威工作副本，同时仍允许其他工作树通过分离 HEAD 或不同分支安全地引用相同提交。</p> <p><a id=\"worktree-cleanup\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_8\">清理工作树</h3> <p>工作树可能占用大量磁盘空间。每个工作树都有自己的一套仓库文件、依赖项、构建缓存等。因此，ChatGPT 桌面应用会尽量将工作树数量控制在合理范围内。</p> <p>默认情况下，Codex 会保留最近使用的 15 个 Codex 托管工作树。如果你希望自行管理磁盘空间，可以在设置中更改此上限或关闭自动删除。</p> <p>Codex 会尽量避免删除仍然重要的工作树。在以下情况下，Codex 托管工作树不会被自动删除：</p> <ul> <li>它关联着已固定的对话。</li> <li>对话仍在进行中。</li> <li>它是永久工作树。</li> </ul> <p>在以下情况下，Codex 托管工作树会被自动删除：</p> <ul> <li>你归档了关联的对话。</li> <li>为符合你设置的数量上限，Codex 需要删除较早的工作树。</li> </ul> <p>删除 Codex 托管工作树前，Codex 会保存其中工作的快照。如果工作树被删除后你重新打开关联的对话，就会看到恢复工作树的选项。</p> <p><a id=\"frequently-asked-questions\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_9\">常见问题</h2> <p><a id=\"-1\"></a> <strong>我可以控制工作树的创建位置吗？</strong></p> <p>可以。默认情况下，Codex 会在 <code>$CODEX_HOME/worktrees</code> 下创建托管工作树。要选择其他位置，请打开 <strong>Settings（设置）&gt; Worktrees（工作树）</strong>，然后更改 <strong>Worktree root（工作树根目录）</strong>。</p> <p><a id=\"can-i-move-a-chat-between-local-and-worktree\"></a></p> <p><a id=\"local-worktree\"></a> <strong>我可以在 Local（本地）与 Worktree（工作树）之间移动对话吗？</strong></p> <p>可以。在对话标题栏中使用 <strong>Hand off（移交）</strong>，即可在本地检出与工作树之间移动对话。Codex 会处理必要的 Git 操作，确保对话在不同环境之间安全移动。如果之后你把对话移回工作树，Codex 会将它返回到同一个关联工作树。</p> <p><a id=\"what-happens-to-chats-if-a-worktree-is-deleted\"></a></p> <p><a id=\"-2\"></a> <strong>工作树被删除后，对话会怎样？</strong></p> <p>即使底层工作树目录已删除，对话仍可能保留在历史记录中。对于 Codex 托管工作树，Codex 会在删除前保存快照；如果你重新打开关联对话，还会提供恢复选项。归档对话时，永久工作树不会被自动删除。</p>","en":"<h1 id=\"worktrees\">Worktrees</h1> <blockquote> <p>For the complete documentation index, see <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt (online)</a>. Markdown versions of documentation pages are available by appending <code>.md</code> to the page URL.</p> </blockquote> <p>Worktrees let Codex run multiple independent chats in the same project without interfering with each other. The repository, worktree, and commands remain on the computer or remote development environment that contains the project. You can work directly in the ChatGPT desktop app, or use <a href=\"#/article/a-e49d01f23299a44c\">Codex</a> in the ChatGPT mobile app to start, guide, approve, and review worktree chats on a connected computer.</p> <p>For Git repositories, <a href=\"#/article/a-b5406e60cbfa0b68\">scheduled tasks</a> can run on dedicated background worktrees so they don't conflict with your ongoing work. In non-version-controlled projects, scheduled tasks run directly in the project directory. You can also start chats in a worktree manually and use Handoff to move a chat between Local and Worktree.</p> <p>Worktrees don't run locally on your phone. The ChatGPT mobile app controls Codex on your connected computer, where the repository and worktree remain, or in the remote development environment that computer uses. The desktop-specific instructions below apply on the connected computer.</p> <h2 id=\"whats-a-worktree\">What's a worktree</h2> <p>Worktrees only work in projects that are part of a Git repository since they use <a href=\"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-worktree\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Git worktrees (online)</a> under the hood. A worktree allows you to create a second copy (\"checkout\") of your repository. Each worktree has its own copy of every file in your repo but they all share the same metadata (<code>.git</code> folder) about commits, branches, etc. This allows you to check out and work on multiple branches in parallel.</p> <h2 id=\"terminology\">Terminology</h2> <ul> <li><strong>Local checkout</strong>: The repository that you created. Sometimes just referred to as <strong>Local</strong> in the ChatGPT desktop app.</li> <li><strong>Worktree</strong>: A <a href=\"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-worktree\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Git worktree (online)</a> that was created from your local checkout in the ChatGPT desktop app.</li> <li><strong>Handoff</strong>: The flow that moves a chat between Local and Worktree. Codex handles the Git operations required to move your work safely between them.</li> </ul> <h2 id=\"why-use-a-worktree\">Why use a worktree</h2> <ol> <li>Work in parallel with Codex without disturbing your current Local setup.</li> <li>Queue up background work while you stay focused on the foreground.</li> <li>Move a chat into Local later when you're ready to inspect, test, or collaborate more directly.</li> </ol> <h2 id=\"getting-started\">Getting started</h2> <p>Worktrees require a Git repository. Make sure the project you selected lives in one.</p> <ol> <li>Select \"Worktree\"</li> </ol> <p>In the new chat view, select <strong>Worktree</strong> under the composer. Optionally, choose a <a href=\"#/article/a-86cde8be2e24d05a\">local environment</a> to run setup scripts for the worktree.</p> <ol> <li>Select the starting branch</li> </ol> <p>Below the composer, choose the Git branch to base the worktree on. This can be your <code>main</code> / <code>master</code> branch, a feature branch, or your current branch with unstaged local changes.</p> <ol> <li>Submit your prompt</li> </ol> <p>Submit your prompt, and Codex creates a Git worktree based on the branch you selected. By default, Codex works in a <a href=\"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-checkout#_detached_head\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">\"detached HEAD\" (online)</a>.</p> <ol> <li>Choose where to keep working</li> </ol> <p>When you're ready, you can either keep working directly on the worktree or hand the chat off to your local checkout. Handing off to or from Local moves your chat <em>and</em> code so you can continue in the other checkout.</p> <h2 id=\"working-between-local-and-worktree\">Working between Local and Worktree</h2> <p>Worktrees look and feel much like your local checkout. The difference is where they fit into your flow. You can think of Local as the foreground and Worktree as the background. Handoff lets you move a chat between them.</p> <p>Under the hood, Handoff handles the Git operations required to move work between two checkouts safely. This matters because <strong>Git only allows a branch to be checked out in one place at a time</strong>. If you check out a branch on a worktree, you <strong>can't</strong> check it out in your local checkout at the same time, and vice versa.</p> <p>In practice, there are two common paths:</p> <ol> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-d955137d94c4d306?anchor=option-1-working-on-the-worktree\">Work exclusively on the worktree</a>. This path works best when you can verify changes directly on the worktree, for example because you have dependencies and tools installed using a <a href=\"#/article/a-86cde8be2e24d05a\">local environment setup script</a>.</li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-d955137d94c4d306?anchor=option-2-handing-a-chat-off-to-local\">Hand the chat off to Local</a>. Use this when you want to bring the chat into the foreground, for example because you want to inspect changes in your usual IDE or can run only one instance of your app.</li> </ol> <h3 id=\"option-1-working-on-the-worktree\">Option 1: Working on the worktree</h3> <p>If you want to stay exclusively on the worktree with your changes, turn your worktree into a branch using the <strong>Create branch here</strong> button in the chat header.</p> <p>From here you can commit your changes, push your branch to your remote repository, and open a pull request on GitHub.</p> <p>You can open your IDE to the worktree using the \"Open\" button in the header, use the integrated terminal, or anything else that you need to do from the worktree directory.</p> <blockquote> <p>Illustration: Worktree chat view with branch controls and worktree details</p> </blockquote> <p>Remember, if you create a branch on a worktree, you can't check it out in any other worktree, including your local checkout.</p> <p><a id=\"option-2-handing-a-thread-off-to-local\"></a> <a id=\"option-2-handing-a-chat-off-to-local\"></a> <a id=\"option-2-handing-a-task-off-to-local\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"option-2-handing-a-chat-off-to-local\">Option 2: Handing a chat off to Local</h3> <p>If you want to bring a chat into the foreground, select <strong>Hand off</strong> in the chat header and move it to <strong>Local</strong>.</p> <p>This path works well when you want to read the changes in your usual IDE window, run your existing development server, or validate the work in the same environment you already use day to day.</p> <p>Codex handles the Git steps required to move the chat safely between the worktree and your local checkout.</p> <p>Each chat keeps the same associated worktree over time. If you hand the chat back to a worktree later, Codex returns it to that same background environment so you can pick up where you left off.</p> <blockquote> <p>Illustration: Handoff dialog moving a chat from a worktree to Local</p> </blockquote> <p>You can also go the other direction. If you're already working in Local and want to free up the foreground, use <strong>Hand off</strong> to move the chat to a worktree. This is useful when you want Codex to keep working in the background while you switch your attention back to something else locally.</p> <p>Since Handoff uses Git operations, any files that are part of your <code>.gitignore</code> file won't move with the chat unless Codex copies them into a local managed worktree with <code>.worktreeinclude</code>.</p> <h2 id=\"advanced-details\">Advanced details</h2> <h3 id=\"codex-managed-and-permanent-worktrees\">Codex-managed and permanent worktrees</h3> <p>By default, chats use a Codex-managed worktree. These are meant to feel lightweight and disposable. A Codex-managed worktree is typically dedicated to one chat, and Codex returns that chat to the same worktree if you hand it back there later.</p> <p>If you want a long-lived environment, create a permanent worktree from the three-dot menu on a project in the sidebar. This creates a new permanent worktree as its own project. Permanent worktrees aren't automatically deleted, and you can start multiple chats from the same worktree.</p> <h3 id=\"how-codex-manages-worktrees-for-you\">How Codex manages worktrees for you</h3> <p>Codex creates worktrees in <code>$CODEX_HOME/worktrees</code>. The starting commit is the <code>HEAD</code> commit of the branch selected when you start your chat. If you chose a branch with local changes, Codex applies the uncommitted changes to the worktree as well. The worktree isn't checked out as a branch. It's in a <a href=\"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-checkout#_detached_head\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">detached HEAD (online)</a> state. This lets Codex create several worktrees without polluting your branches.</p> <h3 id=\"copy-ignored-local-files-into-managed-worktrees\">Copy ignored local files into managed worktrees</h3> <p>Local Codex-managed worktrees start from a Git checkout, so tracked files are already present. If your repository ignores local setup files that a new worktree needs, add a <code>.worktreeinclude</code> file to the repository root and list the ignored paths or <code>.gitignore</code>-style patterns to copy when Codex creates a managed worktree.</p> <p>Use this for files Git intentionally ignores, such as <code>.env</code>, <code>.env.local</code>, or <code>config/secrets.json</code>. Codex only copies ignored files that match <code>.worktreeinclude</code>; it doesn't copy other local files that Git doesn't track. Don't list tracked files.</p> <p>Codex automatically copies an ignored <code>AGENTS.override.md</code> into local managed worktrees, so you don't need to list it in <code>.worktreeinclude</code>.</p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\"># .worktreeinclude\n.env\n.env.local\nconfig/secrets.json\n</code></pre> <p>Codex skips source symlinks and won't overwrite files that already exist in the new checkout. This behavior applies to local ChatGPT desktop app managed worktrees, not remote worktrees or Git worktrees you create yourself from the command line.</p> <h3 id=\"branch-limitations\">Branch limitations</h3> <p>Suppose Codex finishes some work on a worktree and you choose to create a <code>feature/a</code> branch on it using <strong>Create branch here</strong>. Now, you want to try it on your local checkout. If you tried to check out the branch, you would get the following error:</p> <pre><code>fatal: 'feature/a' is already used by worktree at '&lt;WORKTREE_PATH&gt;'\n</code></pre> <p>To resolve this, you would need to check out another branch instead of <code>feature/a</code> on the worktree.</p> <p>If you plan on checking out the branch locally, use Handoff to move the chat into Local instead of trying to keep the same branch checked out in both places at once.</p> <p><a id=\"why-this-limitation-exists\"></a> <strong>Why this limitation exists</strong></p> <p>Git prevents the same branch from being checked out in more than one worktree at a time because a branch represents a single mutable reference (<code>refs/heads/&lt;name&gt;</code>) whose meaning is “the current checked-out state” of a working tree.</p> <p>When a branch is checked out, Git treats its HEAD as owned by that worktree and expects operations like commits, resets, rebases, and merges to advance that reference in a well-defined, serialized way. Allowing multiple worktrees to simultaneously check out the same branch would create ambiguity and race conditions around which worktree’s operations update the branch reference, potentially leading to lost commits, inconsistent indexes, or unclear conflict resolution.</p> <p>By enforcing a one-branch-per-worktree rule, Git guarantees that each branch has a single authoritative working copy, while still allowing other worktrees to safely reference the same commits via detached HEADs or separate branches.</p> <h3 id=\"worktree-cleanup\">Worktree cleanup</h3> <p>Worktrees can take up a lot of disk space. Each one has its own set of repository files, dependencies, build caches, etc. As a result, the ChatGPT desktop app tries to keep the number of worktrees to a reasonable limit.</p> <p>By default, Codex keeps your most recent 15 Codex-managed worktrees. You can change this limit or turn off automatic deletion in settings if you prefer to manage disk usage yourself.</p> <p>Codex tries to avoid deleting worktrees that are still important. Codex-managed worktrees won't be deleted automatically if:</p> <ul> <li>A pinned chat is tied to it</li> <li>The chat is still in progress</li> <li>The worktree is a permanent worktree</li> </ul> <p>Codex-managed worktrees are deleted automatically when:</p> <ul> <li>You archive the associated chat</li> <li>Codex needs to delete older worktrees to stay within your configured limit</li> </ul> <p>Before deleting a Codex-managed worktree, Codex saves a snapshot of the work on it. If you open a chat after its worktree was deleted, you'll see the option to restore it.</p> <h2 id=\"frequently-asked-questions\">Frequently asked questions</h2> <p><a id=\"can-i-control-where-worktrees-are-created\"></a> <strong>Can I control where worktrees are created?</strong></p> <p>Yes. Codex creates managed worktrees under <code>$CODEX_HOME/worktrees</code> by default. To choose another location, open <strong>Settings &gt; Worktrees</strong> and change <strong>Worktree root</strong>.</p> <p><a id=\"can-i-move-a-chat-between-local-and-worktree\"></a></p> <p><a id=\"can-i-move-a-chat-between-local-and-worktree\"></a> <strong>Can I move a chat between Local and Worktree?</strong></p> <p>Yes. Use <strong>Hand off</strong> in the chat header to move a chat between your local checkout and a worktree. Codex handles the Git operations needed to move the chat safely between environments. If you hand a chat back to a worktree later, Codex returns it to the same associated worktree.</p> <p><a id=\"what-happens-to-chats-if-a-worktree-is-deleted\"></a></p> <p><a id=\"what-happens-to-chats-if-a-worktree-is-deleted\"></a> <strong>What happens to chats if a worktree is deleted?</strong></p> <p>Chats can remain in your history even if the underlying worktree directory is deleted. For Codex-managed worktrees, Codex saves a snapshot before deleting the worktree and offers to restore it if you reopen the associated chat. Permanent worktrees are not automatically deleted when you archive their chats.</p>"};
